#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059487 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "50c01507-99a7-5328-8d74-c9e9bda51330" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix Using the Matrix of Cofactors"}] :question/id #uuid "8cd94856-fbe5-53a4-b7f2-f5b29b7aa927" :question/math-academy-id "e-4514" :question/problem "Find the inverse of $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the determinant of $A$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A) =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 8\\end{vmatrix} &= 0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{vmatrix}- 1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{vmatrix}+ 2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 0 - (8 - 12) + 2(-3 - 0) \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A$.\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= [\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\mid \\mid 1\\begin{vmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{vmatrix}8 \\mid \\mid \\mid 1\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\\\ 4\\end{vmatrix}- 3 \\mid \\\\\n&= \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\mid \\mid 0\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{vmatrix}8 \\mid \\mid \\mid 0\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{vmatrix}- 3 \\mid \\\\\n&= \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\mid \\mid 0\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}3 \\mid \\mid \\mid 0\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}0 \\mid ] \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}9 & -4 & -3 \\\\ 14 & -8 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}9 & - (-4) & -3 \\\\ - (14) & -8 & - (-4) \\\\ 3 & - (-2) & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{-1} &= (1)/(det(A))C^{T} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{-2}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{9}{2} & 7 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & -2 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059488 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d79fb5d1-22a6-511d-90ca-7d66c540d172" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cofactor Method To Solve for an Unknown Quantity"}] :question/id #uuid "9180ff3b-46d2-5df7-81b6-ac75bef51e00" :question/math-academy-id "e-4515" :question/problem "The entry $a'_{31}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nequals $1$. Find the value of $k$." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A) =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 3\\end{vmatrix} &= 3\\begin{vmatrix}k & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}- 2\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+ (-2)\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & k \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 3(3k - 0) - 2(-6 - 0) - 2(6 + k) \\\\\n&= 9k + 12 - 12 - 2k \\\\\n&= 7k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry $a'_{31}$ in $A^{-1}$, we need to know the value of $c_{13}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nc_{13} &= (-1)^{1 + 3}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & k \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & k \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 6 + k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since\n\n$$\nA^{-1}=\\dfrac{1}{\\det (A)}C^{T}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{31}^{′} &= (1)/(det(A))c_{13} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{7k} \\cdot (6 + k) \\\\\n&= \\frac{6 + k}{7k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that $a'_{31}=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{6 + k}{7k} &= 1 \\\\\n6 + k &= 7k \\\\\n6 &= 6k \\\\\nk &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059489 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "26e9a55f-7711-5d8f-ab0a-e8638f2d2dec" :question/math-academy-id "e-4516" :question/problem "Determine the entry $a'_{23}$ in the inverse of the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A) =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} &= -2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}- 3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+ (-3)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=-2(2 - 0) - 3(0 - 0) - 3(0 - 1) \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry $a'_{23}$ in $A^{-1}$, we need to know the value of $c_{32}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nc_{32} &= (-1)^{3 + 2}M_{32} \\\\\n&= (-1)^{5}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=-1 \\cdot (0) \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since\n\n$$\nA^{-1}=\\dfrac{1}{\\det (A)}C^{T}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\na'_{23} = \\dfrac{1}{-1}c_{32} = -1\\cdot 0 = 0\n$$"}]}
